ED_DecisionText:
At its 93rd plenary meeting, on 25 June 2026, the General Assembly, on the proposal of Belize, Morocco, Saint Kitts and Nevis, Suriname and Vanuatu, (Footnote: A/80/L.76.) recalling its resolutions 77/318 of 25 July 2023 and 79/316 of 30 June 2025, by which it had requested the Secretary-General to convene, within existing resources, in 2025, a global conference on promoting interreligious and intercultural dialogue and tolerance in countering hate speech that involves United Nations entities, Member States, political figures, religious leaders, faith based organizations, media, civil society and other relevant stakeholders, noting that the conference could not be convened in 2025 owing to the ongoing liquidity constraints of the Organization, and taking note of the proposal of the Secretary-General contained in his letter dated 8 May 2026 addressed to the President of the General Assembly, circulated by the President to all Permanent Representatives and Permanent Observers to the United Nations on 13 May 2026:  

Decided that the aforementioned global conference shall be convened on 17 September 2026, prior to the General Assembly high-level week;   

Expressed its appreciation to the Kingdom of Morocco, which presented and co-sponsored resolutions 77/318 and 79/316, for its generosity and continued commitment and willingness to provide financial support related to the conference.
